---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Utilizing Git - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="utilizing-git">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Git is your most important safety net when coding with AI. AI tools generate a lot of code quickly, and not all of it will be right. Git lets you
				move fast, try things, and confidently roll back when something goes sideways.
			</p>

			<h3>Commit After Every Prompt</h3>

			<p>
				The single most useful habit you can build: <strong>commit after every successful prompt cycle</strong>. You give the AI a task, it makes changes,
				you verify the changes work, you commit. Then you move to the next task. This creates natural checkpoints you can return to at any point.
			</p>

			<p>
				AI tools sometimes go down a bad path. Maybe the third prompt in a series introduces a regression, or the AI starts
				"fixing" things that weren't broken. Without frequent commits, your only option is to ask the AI to undo its changes, which wastes time and
				tokens, and often makes things worse. The AI doesn't reliably remember what it changed or why. Note: some tools have checkpointing capabilities that can help with this; but Git is still the most reliable way to go.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					When something goes wrong, don't ask the AI to undo it. Just run <code>git checkout .</code> or{" "}
					<code>git reset --hard HEAD</code> to get back to your last good state. It's instant, reliable, and
					costs zero tokens. Then re-think your prompt and requirements and start over.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Know When to Throw It Away</h3>

			<p>
				Sometimes the AI goes far enough down the wrong path that the best move is to scrap the whole attempt and start fresh. This feels wasteful, but
				it's often faster than trying to course-correct. You learned something from the failed attempt, so your next prompt will be better.
			</p>

			<p>
				A good rule of thumb: if you've spent more than two prompts trying to get the AI to fix its own output, reset to your last commit and rethink
				your approach. The time you "lose" is almost always less than the time you'd spend wrestling with compounding mistakes.
			</p>

			<p>
				Also consider whether the task is just too difficult for the AI. Some changes require deep understanding of the system's behavior, subtle
				trade-offs, or context that's hard to convey in a prompt. If you've reset and re-prompted with clearer instructions and it's still not
				landing, that's a signal to just write the code yourself. You'll often finish faster, and you'll understand the change better when it's
				time to review.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-2">The workflow</div>
					<ol class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5 m-0">
						<li>Give the AI a well-scoped task</li>
						<li>Review the changes (diff, run tests, check the UI)</li>
						<li>If it works: commit and move on</li>
						<li>If it doesn't: <code>git checkout .</code> and re-prompt with better context</li>
					</ol>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Conventional Commits</h3>

			<p>
				If you're committing frequently, your Git history becomes a form of documentation.{" "}
				<a href="https://www.conventionalcommits.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Conventional commits</a>{" "}
				make that documentation actually useful. Instead of a log full of "fix stuff" and "update code", you get structured messages that the AI
				itself can use as context. When the AI reads your Git log, well-written conventional commits help it understand what has changed recently,
				what areas of the codebase are active, and what patterns the team follows.
			</p>

			<p>
				If you include ticket numbers in your commits, this can also assist in linking the commit to the actual ticket, allowing the AI to follow the commit back to the actual ticket, read the requirements, and understand the full context behind the change.
			</p>

			<p>
				AI tools are great at writing these messages for you. You can create a{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-writing/skills">skill</a> that teaches your AI tool to follow
				this format automatically. We have a{" "}
				<a
					href="/downloads/conventional-commits-skill.zip"
				>conventional commits skill</a> you can download and modify to fit your project.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Advanced: parallel work with worktrees">
				<p>
					Once you're comfortable with the basics, <strong>Git worktrees</strong> are worth learning. A worktree is a separate checkout of your
					repo on its own branch, so a tool can make changes there without touching your working directory. That lets you keep coding while an
					agent works in parallel, or run several agents at once without their edits colliding. This has matured from an experiment into a
					supported feature: Claude Code has built-in worktree support (<code>claude --worktree &lt;name&gt;</code>, and{" "}
					<code>isolation: worktree</code> for sub-agents), and Cursor gives each parallel agent its own worktree.
				</p>
				<p>
					It adds setup and review overhead, so reach for it when you're genuinely running more than one task at a time. To go deeper, see Claude Code's{" "}
					<a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/worktrees" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">guide to running parallel sessions with worktrees</a>{" "}
					and the underlying{" "}
					<a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">git worktree reference</a>.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
